<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wei Zifu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 91 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Empress Xiaowusi, Thoughtful Empress Wei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wei Zifu was an empress consort of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, the second wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu</a>, his spouse for 49 years and his empress for 38, the second longest tenure in Chinese history. Born to a serf family in the household of Princess Pingyang, where she was trained as a singer and dancer, she caught the emperor's eye in 139 BCE and was made empress after bearing his first son, Liu Ju. Her half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wei-qing">Wei Qing</a> and nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huo-qubing">Huo Qubing</a> became leading generals. In 91 BCE, amid the witchcraft persecutions, the official Jiang Chong accused the crown prince; Liu Ju rose against the conspirators with her support and was defeated, and when the emperor had her seal seized she took her own life.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Wei Qing, the son of a servant woman in a princess’s household, worked as a sheepherder and stableboy before his half-sister <b>Wei Zifu</b> became Emperor Wu’s empress; he rose to be Grand General and led the armies against the Xiongnu.</blockquote><a href="/societies/han-china">Social Hierarchy of Han China</a></li></ul></section>
